never bring up your king in time: so it must be a drawn-game. It is proper to observe, that the
pawns of the two bishops, and of the two rooks, at a square of the queen, make a drawn-game,
against a queen; and the pawns of the king, the queen, and of the two knights, must lose in such
a position, 4.5 1.6} *
